(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-acute-vision-loss-quiz"]={"title":"Acute Vision Loss Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 51-year-old man is diagnosed with non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. Which age range does the lecture give?","choices":["40 to 60 years","55 years and older","18 to 45 years","Under 30 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, accounting for 90 to 95 percent of cases. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is being worked up for amaurosis fugax. Which two embolic sources does the lecture name?","choices":["Aortic and femoral","Carotid and cardiac","Pulmonary and renal","Vertebral and basilar"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with retinal vascular spasm as a third mechanism. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man asks what separates the two forms of anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. What does the lecture give?","choices":["Which eye is affected","Whether vision loss is painful","Whether intraocular pressure is raised","Whether giant cell arteritis is present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; the arteritic form is caused by it. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has bayoneting noted on her optic disc. What does the term describe?","choices":["Pallor of the whole disc","Blood vessels with narrow angulations","Widening of the central cup","Haemorrhage in all four quadrants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, one of the glaucomatous rim changes. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has chronic open-angle glaucoma. Which optic nerve findings does the lecture list besides cupping?","choices":["An elevated grey retina with folds","Rim pitting, bayoneting and splinter haemorrhages","Cotton wool spots, venous dilation and haemorrhage","Engorged veins with disc elevation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with rim thinning and visual field defects. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man says a curtain came down over his vision. Why does the lecture warn about this description?","choices":["It is never a real symptom","It always means glaucoma","It indicates raised intracranial pressure","More than one condition uses it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; it fits amaurosis fugax and retinal detachment. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with optic neuritis asks how long recovery takes. What does the lecture describe?","choices":["No recovery at any point","Recovery within hours","Gradual decline over years","Improvement within weeks, normal vision within a year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, often without treatment. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man presents with acute angle closure. Which topical agents does the lecture instil first?","choices":["Atropine drops","Pilocarpine or timolol","Latanoprost or tafluprost","Prednisolone drops"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, a topical alpha-blocker or beta-blocker. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 80-year-old woman is treated for arteritic optic neuropathy. What does the lecture say the outlook depends on?","choices":["Whether surgery was performed","The number of demyelinating lesions","The type of detachment","Duration of symptoms and when steroids began"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why early therapy is critical. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man is being treated for arteritic optic neuropathy. Why does the lecture describe it as a medical emergency?","choices":["It detaches the retina","Untreated, it leads to blindness","It causes a stroke within hours","It raises intraocular pressure acutely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the second eye is at risk. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with optic neuritis asks why she needs a brain MRI when her sight is improving. What is the reason?","choices":["To exclude a carotid embolus","To grade the optic nerve cupping","To find an underlying cause such as multiple sclerosis","To measure the intraocular pressure in both eyes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; treating the episode alone can miss a larger problem. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man is being taught how to separate papilledema from glaucoma at the optic disc. What is the key difference?","choices":["Neither changes the disc","Only glaucoma affects the appearance of the disc","Papilledema pushes the disc out; glaucoma cups it in","Both cup the disc equally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the lecturer stressed how distinct they look. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man asks what the definitive treatment for glaucoma is. What does the lecture state?","choices":["Surgery","Lifelong eye drops","Oral acetazolamide","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, for both the acute and chronic forms. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has a retinal detachment. What timeframe does the lecture give for surgical repair?","choices":["At the next routine clinic","Immediately in every case, without exception","Urgently, or within a week depending on type","Only if vision fails to improve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man asks how serious glaucoma is worldwide. What did the lecturer say?","choices":["It rarely causes blindness","It is confined to one population group","It is always reversible","It is a leading cause of blindness worldwide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and untreated disease ends in blindness. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with optic neuritis is found to have multiple sclerosis. Which treatment does the lecture mention?","choices":["Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Antiplatelet therapy","Calcium channel blockers","Corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, considered when a demyelinating cause is identified. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has acute papilledema. Which findings distinguish it from the chronic phase?","choices":["A cherry-red spot","Haemorrhages and cotton wool spots","Disc elevation alone","Loss of the optic nerve axons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; the chronic phase lacks both. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman is diagnosed with arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. What causes it?","choices":["Carotid embolism","Raised intracranial pressure","Giant cell arteritis","A small crowded optic disc"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, also called temporal arteritis. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man describes vision descending over one eye like a shade from above. What does the lecture attribute this to?","choices":["Retinal ischemia","Raised intracranial pressure","Optic nerve demyelination","Vitreous traction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, when the loss descends over the field of one eye. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has amaurosis fugax. How long does the lecture say an episode lasts?","choices":["A few seconds to minutes","One to two days","Instantaneous and permanent","Weeks to months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; hours argues against a transient ischemic attack. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has optic neuritis. Which causes does the lecture give?","choices":["Vitamin A toxicity","Multiple sclerosis, autoimmune, postviral or idiopathic","Embolism arising from the carotid artery or heart","Thrombosis of the retinal vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has chronic open-angle glaucoma. How does the lecture describe the typical presentation?","choices":["Asymptomatic in most patients","Severe eye pain and vomiting","Sudden painless loss over seconds","Painful eye movement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; peripheral field is lost slowly and silently. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman is told she has one of the three types of retinal detachment. Which types does the lecture name?","choices":["Arteritic and non-arteritic types","Open-angle and closed-angle","Acute, chronic and atrophic","Rhegmatogenous, traction and serous"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; the serous form is also called exudative. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a central retinal artery occlusion. Which etiologies does the lecture list?","choices":["Raised intracranial pressure","Arteriosclerosis, atherosclerosis and emboli","Venous thrombosis","Optic nerve demyelination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, the emboli being carotid or cardiac. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has secondary angle-closure glaucoma. Which mechanical causes does the lecture list?","choices":["Demyelination and inflammation","Vitreous shrinkage and traction on the retina","Tumour, scarring or other mechanical causes","Embolism and thrombosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has a central retinal artery occlusion. Why does the lecture perform anterior chamber paracentesis?","choices":["To lower the intraocular pressure","To deliver a drug into the retina","To reattach the retina mechanically","To measure intracranial pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, fluid is withdrawn from the eye with a needle. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man has a central retinal artery occlusion. Which fundus findings does the lecture describe?","choices":["Pale retinal swelling with a cherry-red fovea","Disc swelling with cotton wool spots and haemorrhage","A deeply cupped disc","Bilateral disc elevation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with emboli visible in the central artery. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man needs retinal detachment repair. Which of these does the lecture list among the surgical options?","choices":["Carotid endarterectomy","Anterior chamber paracentesis","Pneumatic retinopexy","Peripheral iridotomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with cryotherapy, vitrectomy, laser photocoagulation and scleral buckle. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has papilledema. Which non-visual symptoms does the lecture list?","choices":["Nausea, vomiting and headache","Jaw claudication and scalp tenderness","Fever and night sweats","Neck stiffness alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, the non-specific signs of raised intracranial pressure. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has amaurosis fugax. What does the lecture identify as the core of management?","choices":["Start high-dose corticosteroids","Observe without intervention","Treat the underlying cause","Lower the intraocular pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; the lecturer called this the essential point. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
